 so  there are tons of people who are using  marijuana right now as a medicine to  treat a whole number of issues from  cancer to pain to epilepsy you you hear  these stories all the time and it's it's  shocking that the DEA and the Department  of Health and Human Services for some  reason isn't understanding this there's  this insane vicious circle that exists  where most of those politicians that are  against loosening the restrictions  around this type of research and around  legalization and decriminalization as  well as the DEA and the FDA etc they all  say we're totally open to having our  minds changed about cannabis about  marijuana  we just want scientifically valid and  well controlled clinical trials done  under the investigational new drug  application paradigm the problem is that  that's a paradigm that makes it very  difficult to really do a lot of research  which in turn will make it so that we  never actually get the loosening of  restrictions it's a circle that is  completely closed and to me makes  absolutely no sense for anybody who says  I'm completely open to having my mind  changed on this here's the mechanism  through which my mind will be changed  and it's an incredibly restrictive  mechanism the University of Mississippi  right now has permission from the US  government to do this sort of research  on marijuana and this whole approach  that you just broke down it makes it  seem like this isn't settled science  like there's still a doubt whether or  not marijuana can be an effective drug  to treat a number of ailments in this  country when it is settled science in 25  states and for more than a million  people are using it and just there was  research done on Medicare Part D  recipients and they found that because a  number of Medicare Part this is a  prescription drug program a number of  people on the program are switching from  traditional painkillers and using  medicinal marijuana which has in turn  save the government something like two  hundred and thirty million dollars  recently so the effects of this not just  the people who are using it  prove that this has medicinal value but  it's also saving the government money in  the long run as more people used  marijuana to treat their elements rather  than the pharmaceutical company's  offerings and if you look at who's  behind I think this was an investigation  done by lee fong over at the intercept a  year or two ago a lot of the forces that  are lobbying against marijuana  legalization at the state level you know  it comes on a lot of ballot initiatives  or pharmaceutical companies so they have  an interest at stake here and that's  what could be one reason why you don't  see the the federal government and  policy makers moving as quickly as the  rest of the country seems to be moving  on this issue and that sort of connects  to another area where there's a huge  double standard which is that I can't  think of a sort of logically consistent  case for cigarettes and alcohol to be  legal and regulated and taxed but not  marijuana period I mean I feel like I  can say much more about that but I don't  know that I need to I I have not heard a  compelling case for why cigarettes and  alcohol should be handled the way they  are and for mayor  marijuana to be a schedule 1 controlled  substance I haven't heard one either  other than a more cynical argument of  the influence of these industries but of  the tobacco and alcohol industries but  even just just look at what you hear on  the campaign trail or we're hearing it  suppose you're not hearing much about it  anymore but this is the opioid abuse  epidemic that's hitting the country this  is a serious problem all around all  around the country you heard politicians  talking about it and not a single person  has ever died from using marijuana and  yet we're hooking people on all these  painkillers that are proving to be very  deadly for people ruining people's lives  when there are medical alternatives to a  lot of these drugs in marijuana if if  the government and policymakers would  get on board with this I mean are as I  mentioned half the country argued or  half the states in this country already  are on board with it and over a million  people already are on board with it as  well but there are serious problems with  a lot of drugs that we allow  that aren't considered schedule 1 drugs  but our farm were destructive than  marijuana not to mention a lot of those  schedule to painkillers are one of the  actual leading gateways when prescribed  by doctors that will get people hooked  on the Schedule one drug of heroin  because it ends up being much cheaper to  buy heroin on the street than to try to  convince your doctor to keep giving you  schedule 2 drug prescriptions or buying  those pills on the streets that actually  is a gateway mechanism that's  established and that that also seems to  be sort of missing from the context of  the marijuana discussion and here in  Massachusetts that's incredibly visible  as I know you know speaking of  Massachusetts there is going to be a  vote here a ballot question in November  again on on on marijuana do you believe  that those ballot initiatives and what  we've seen happen in some states are  actually going to turn the tide  federally on the status of marijuana I  mean so far there has been some pretty  significant resistance at the federal  level despite what happens in some  states without much of a choice on  matter you're right there has been a  reluctance on the part of the federal  government to to reclassify marijuana or  to just you know drop the laws from the  books and the prohibition laws from the  books but they have reacted to a lot of  these states that have chosen to  legalize marijuana recreationally by  telling their agents basically to stand  down we have of course seen some growers  and some establishments busted up but  for the most part people are allowed to  operate in a lot of these states as long  as they're operating in accordance with  the law free from a lot of federal  government intervention I think  President Obama's Justice Department is  is respecting that somewhat mainly  because you know is that really the  fight that the Justice Department wants  to take on right now given all the other  issues about prisons and you know we  imprison the most people anywhere in the  world I don't think we need to go around  busting more pot heads and they don't  think President Obama's Justice  Department is interest in that having  said that I could easily see a different  Justice Department even a  a Donald Trump Justice Department who's  a guy who's going around to having  himself as the law and order candidate  deciding that he they don't care what  all these states are doing I mean the  federal government still has is well  within its power since marijuana is  still against federal law to crack down  on all this stuff but I think the  smarter approach which which the Obama  Justice Department is sort of taken is  stand down and let these states do do  what they want last thing I want to  touch on Sam there are some who are  opposed to both the decriminalization  and legalization of recreational  marijuana who say once you open that  door there's no way to justify keeping  any drug either criminalized or or at  least sort of like a civil violation for  its recreational use I tend to resist  the slippery slope arguments unless  there's a really strong compelling case  to be made for it is there one for that  argument or is that does that not really  make sense I don't think it makes much  sense either I mean just the fact that  we we have alcohol and cigarettes legal  and we managed to resist all the rest of  the drugs from being legal also I you  know I'm somewhat sympathetic to the  argument that perhaps all drugs should  be legal I know a number of countries  around the world have experimenting with  this and have seen positive results but  you know that's going maybe pushing it a  little farther than we need to be  pushing it right now this idea of  marijuana being a gateway drug I just I  find it somewhat laughable as though  it's a unique type of gateway drug I  mean I went to college I hung out with  the kids who are doing marijuana and I  hung out with the kids who are doing  harder drugs and you can tell there  wasn't usually a link between those two  types of kids if you want to see what a  what a gateway drug is look at alcohol  and the effects that that hasn't an  individual on how that pushes an  individual to do other drugs and you  know it's just a matter I guess of the  DEA and the Department of Health and  Human Services waking up to this we  there was that infamous clip a few years  ago when jared Polis was questioning the  DEA administrator Michele Leonhart in  which she refused to say whether or not  heroin is a more destructive or more  harmful drug than marijuana yeah well  she's left and we now have Chuck  Rosenberg who is this slightly better he  admitted that heroin heroin is worse  than marijuana but yet he called  medicinal marijuana a joke so only  slightly better there still needs to be  a lot of growing up among among the  leadership over there yeah incremental  improvements I guess Sam sax writer  reporter co-founder of the district  Sentinel Sam great to talk to you thank  you yeah thanks a lot
 

